{
  "result": "I've been thinking about the hours that don't belong to anyone. You know the ones - 3:47 a.m. when you get up for water, or 4:15 p.m. on a Sunday in November when the light has already given up. Those pockets of time that aren't morning, evening, work, or rest. They're just... there.\n\nI like to imagine that all the lost things collect there. Not the big things like lost loves or lost opportunities. The small ones. The other sock. The name that was on the tip of your tongue at the grocery store. The perfect comeback you thought of three hours too late. They all float around in that 3:47 a.m. air, waiting.\n\nMaybe that's why we feel so strange in those hours. We're intruding on a meeting of lost things. The world is holding its breath.\n\nMy grandmother used to say that houses need to settle. She meant the creaks, but I think she also meant that houses need time to be empty, even when people live in them. Time to remember what they are without us.\n\nSo I try to leave some hours alone now. I don't fill every silence with a podcast. I don't check my phone while the kettle boils. I just let the kettle boil. I let the Sunday light fade without trying to photograph it. And for a minute, maybe, I'm not lost or found. I'm just there, in the pocket, with all the other small, wandering things. They keep me quiet company tonight.",
